		<profileDesc>
			<abstract>
				<p>We investigate the effect of duplicating multihead self-attention layers in large language models (LLMs) across a range of language tasks, with and without fine-tuning. The results demonstrate that duplicating the initial layers once or twice often yields a significant performance boost. Attention analysis uncovered the underlying mechanisms driving the improvement when performing layer duplication. This method enhances LLM capabilities with or without additional training or labeled data.</p>
			</abstract>
